Overview
This practical implements a Multilayer Perceptron (MLP) neural network for classifying the Iris dataset. 
The model is evaluated using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score, confusion matrix, loss curve, and actual vs predicted graphs.
Two train-test split scenarios are used to study the effect of different amounts of training data.

Dataset – Iris
The Iris dataset is a commonly used dataset for classification. It contains measurements of iris flowers belonging to three different species.
Total samples: 150
Number of features: 4
Feature values: Sepal length, Sepal width, Petal length, Petal width
Number of classes: 3
Classes: Setosa, Versicolor, Virginica
Each sample contains four numerical measurements, and the target label represents the species of the flower.

Model
The MLP classifier uses:
Hidden layers: 2
Neurons: 10 neurons in each hidden layer
Activation: ReLU
Optimizer: Adam
Maximum iterations: 1000
The input consists of the four Iris features, while the output predicts one of the three flower classes.

Scenarios
Scenario 1
Training data: 80%
Testing data: 20%
Random state: 42
Scenario 2
Training data: 90%
Testing data: 10%
Random state: 42
The same MLP architecture is used in both scenarios so that their performance can be compared fairly.
